 "beats": [
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "scene": "sack",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.35
    ],
    "to": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.15
    ]
   },
   "text": "Rome once had to pay its enemies a thousand pounds of gold."
  },
  {
   "type": "title",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "globe",
   "text": "Did Rome really pay a ransom in gold?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "It did. And Romans never forgot it. Later, they marked the day of the defeat as an unlucky day."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "today",
   "date": "Today",
   "place": "Rome, Italy",
   "text": "So where did it happen?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Right in the city. The last Romans held out on the Capitoline Hill, where Rome's city hall stands today."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "gauls",
   "text": "Who were they fighting?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Gauls. A Celtic tribe called the Senones, from northeast Italy, led by a chief named Brennus."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "date": "390 BC",
   "place": "River Allia",
   "text": "In 390 BC, about sixteen kilometres north of Rome, at the river Allia, they crushed the Roman army.",
   "say": "In three ninety BC, about sixteen kilometres north of Rome, at the river Allia, they crushed the Roman army."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "sack",
   "text": "And the city?",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.55,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.5,
     0.45,
     1.25
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Most of it fell. The Gauls burned houses and temples. Only the Capitoline Hill held out."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "geese",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.5,
     0.45,
     1.3
    ]
   },
   "text": "Is it true geese saved them?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "That's the story. One night the Gauls climbed the cliff, and the sacred geese of the goddess Juno woke the defenders."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "siege",
   "text": "So why pay at all?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "After seven months, both sides were hungry and sick. Rome agreed to pay a thousand pounds of gold."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "scene": "vae",
   "text": "When the Romans said the weights were rigged, Brennus threw his sword on the scale and said: Vae victis. Woe to the conquered.",
   "say": "When the Romans said the weights were rigged, Brennus threw his sword on the scale and said: Why, victis. Woe to the conquered."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "wall",
   "date": "378 BC",
   "place": "Rome",
   "text": "What did Rome do next?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "It built a stone wall around the city, about eleven kilometres long. Pieces of it still stand next to Termini station."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "borders",
   "date": "264 BC",
   "place": "Italy",
   "text": "And did it work?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "No foreign army took Rome again for eight hundred years. And by 264 BC, Rome ruled almost all of Italy.",
   "say": "No foreign army took Rome again for eight hundred years. And by two sixty-four BC, Rome ruled almost all of Italy."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Next time: Hannibal brings elephants over the Alps. Like the video and follow History Sketches so you don't miss it.",
   "cta": true
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "And tell us in the comments: would you have paid?",
   "cta": true
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "scene": "sack",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.15
    ],
    "to": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.35
    ]
   },
   "text": "But first, Rome had to pay a thousand pounds of gold."
  }
